course: dictionary
lesson: 6677
title: Objections
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: An objection is a reason you give for not agreeing with something, or the act of saying out loud that you are against it. «Strong thinkers look for the best objections to their own view, not the weakest.» So a good thinker looks for the strongest reasons someone could give against their idea, and tests the idea against them.
explain: Context helps here too. «You can respond to an objection by showing why it is weaker, or by changing your view.» To respond means to answer, so an objection is something you must answer. You can prove it is weak, or you can change your mind.
explain: Common phrases give more clues. «Have no objection» means you do not mind. «Over someone's objections» means something happened even though they disagreed. «Any objections?» asks whether anyone is against the plan.
explain: In a court, a lawyer says «Objection!» to tell the judge that a question or answer breaks the rules. It is the same word, but now it is a formal way to say stop.
fact: An objection is a reason for not agreeing, or the act of saying you are against something.
fact: «Objections» is the plural, meaning more than one reason or protest.
fact: In a debate or persuasive writing, a strong thinker looks for the best objections to their own view.
fact: «Over someone's objections» means it happened even though they disagreed.
fact: In a court, «Objection!» tells the judge that a question or answer breaks the rules.
exercise: «Strong thinkers look for the best objections to their own view, not the weakest.» What is an objection in this sentence? | A kind of pet | *A reason someone might give against an idea | A happy song | A prize for winning a debate
exercise: «You can respond to an objection by showing why it is weaker, or by changing your view.» What does respond mean here? | *Reply to, or answer | Shout loudly | Ignore it and forget it | Ask for a holiday
exercise: Mia says, «I have no objection to your plan.» What does she mean? | She wants to go to court | *She does not mind the plan | She has a new plan of her own | She hates the plan
test: In a court, what does a lawyer mean by saying «Objection!»? | That the lawyer is hungry | *That the question or answer breaks the rules | That the judge is wrong about the weather | That the court is closed
test: Which phrase means something happened even though people disagreed? | any objections? | have no objection | *over someone's objections | raise a hand
exam: Which best describes what a good thinker does with objections? | *Looks for the strongest objections to their own view | Only listens to the weakest ones | Forgets them straight away | Hides them from everyone
